rmidable Roman forces led by Scipio Africanus, marking a critical juncture in Carthage's defense of its homeland.

Hanno's most notable military involvement occurred during the Roman invasion of Africa. He commanded a substantial force of Carthaginian and Numidian troops, forming a crucial part of Mago Barca's army, which aimed to repel Scipio's advance. His strategic movements and engagements culminated in the disastrous Battle of the Great Plains in 203 BCE. In this decisive confrontation, Scipio employed a cunning maneuver, setting fire to the Carthaginian and Numidian camps at night, followed by a direct assault. Hanno, along with Mago, suffered a crushing defeat, with thousands of their soldiers killed or captured, severely weakening Carthage's ability to resist.

The defeat at the Great Plains, in which Hanno was personally captured by Roman forces, had profound consequences for Carthage. It directly paved the way for Scipio Africanus's continued advance and set the stage for the ultimate Roman victory at the Battle of Zama a year later. Hanno's capture demoralized the Carthaginian leadership and populace, signaling the imminent collapse of their resistance and the end of their ambitions for Mediterranean dominance. His fate underscored the military superiority of Rome in the conflict's final phase."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T15:32:13Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28745721155", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "250 BCE, Carthage"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "250 BCE, Carthage"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Second Punic War necessitated the appointment of numerous Carthaginian generals to command forces across various theaters.", "Mago Barca's command in North Africa required experienced subordinate officers to lead divisions against the Roman invasion.", "Carthage's strategic need to defend its homeland against Scipio Africanus's invasion created opportunities for generals like Hanno to rise to prominence."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-05T15:32:13Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28745721155", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "203 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "203 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Hanno the Elder's defeat at the Battle of the Great Plains significantly weakened Carthaginian military strength in North Africa.", "His capture contributed to the demoralization of Carthaginian force